The page image is almost entirely illegible. It holds a right-hand column of very faded Kurrent prose (about 60 lines), a few short faded marginal passages in the left column (upper left, middle, lower left), and some darker-ink lines at the bottom, several of them struck through. I cannot reliably read any formula or sentence. A faithful step-by-step reconstruction of the page's own content is therefore impossible, and none is offered.

What the context allows me to say (this is my inference, not a reading of the page): the previous page (108r) is a draft of Riemann's natural-philosophy fragment on gravitation. It restates Newton's hypotheses: absolute space; at each ponderable point a cause measured by velocity; at each point of space an accelerating force, the same for all bodies; and an 'absolute Schwerkraft' of each ponderable point producing an inverse-square accelerating force that combines geometrically. Riemann then proposes to seek the accelerating force in the motion of a homogeneous substance filling all space. Its velocity has the direction of the force and a magnitude proportional to it, so it is 'a physical space whose points move in the geometric one'. The previous page ends by starting a computation: decompose the region containing ponderable points into elements ds with gravitational strength dm, and let r be the distance from an arbitrary point O.

The natural continuation, which matches the fragments printed in Riemann's Werke (1876, 'Neue mathematische Principien der Naturphilosophie' / 'Gravitation und Licht'), would be:
- form the potential V(O) = ∫ dm/r;
- note that the accelerating force is -grad V (up to sign and constant) and so has the inverse-square form;
- note that ΔV = 0 outside matter (and ΔV = -4πρ inside);
- interpret the ether velocity field u ∝ grad V as an incompressible flow with sources or sinks at the ponderable points, so that div u ∝ density;
- possibly discuss the flux of the substance through closed surfaces (Gauss).

This cannot be confirmed from the image. The lone legible structure is the two-column layout, with the main text on the right and additions or variants on the left. That layout is consistent with Riemann's drafting habit of placing revisions in the left margin.
